course: dictionary
lesson: 13055
title: Favourable: good for you
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Favourable means good for you, or helpful. It can also mean that someone likes an idea or thinks well of it. The word is spelled favourable in UK English, and favorable in US English. Both spellings mean the same thing.
explain: Look at the words around it. «The wind was favourable, so the boat sailed fast.» A fast boat needs a helpful wind, so favourable means helpful here. «The teacher wrote a favourable report about my story.» A report is what someone writes about your work, so this time favourable shows that the teacher liked it.
explain: Scientists use the word too. Plants and animals need favourable conditions: the right warmth, water and light. Check your meaning by swapping in a word like helpful or good. If the sentence still makes sense, you have found it.
fact: Favourable means good for you, helpful, or giving an advantage.
fact: «A favourable report» means a report that shows approval, so the writer likes what they have seen.
fact: «Favourable conditions» means the right warmth, water and light for plants or animals to grow and live well.
fact: A favourable answer is a yes, and a favourable opinion is a good opinion.
fact: The phrase «favourable to» means helpful or kind to something, as in «The new rule is favourable to young readers.» The opposite is unfavourable.
exercise: «The wind was favourable, so the boat sailed fast.» What does favourable mean here? | Cold and grey | Very windy and dangerous | Slow and quiet | *Helpful for sailing
exercise: The shop gave us a favourable answer. What kind of answer is that? | A long wait | A question | A loud no | *A yes
exercise: «Plants grow well in favourable conditions.» Which conditions help plants? | Total darkness and no water | *The right warmth, water and light | Dry sand with no sun | Freezing cold and strong wind
test: «Her teacher wrote a favourable report about her work.» What does favourable mean in this sentence? | *Showing approval, liking the work | Showing anger | Very short | Very old
test: «Our team had favourable weather for the match.» What does favourable weather mean? | Icy weather that was dangerous | Hot weather that was too strong | Rainy weather that stopped play | *Good weather that helped us
exam: Ben says, «The coach has a favourable view of my running.» What does that show? | The coach is running too | The coach cannot see him | The coach is cross with him | *The coach likes and approves of his running
